# The lint round trip, end to end: index a vault, flag a finding, fix it, and
# come back clean.
#
# This is the one script that drives the whole pipeline — SQLite index, chunker,
# link extractor, lint analysis, and the in-place rewriter — because lint
# reaches all of it without the ONNX model. It is also the shape a pre-commit
# hook uses.

env SEMANTIC_CACHE_DIR=$WORK/cache

# A finding is an error exit, not just a printed list. A hook that runs lint
# has to be able to fail the commit on the status code alone.
#
# --no-embed is passed once, here, and nowhere else: it became a no-op when
# skipping embedding turned into the default, and it stays accepted because
# hooks and CI in other repositories still pass it. This asserts it still
# parses.
! semantic lint --no-embed
stderr 'reindexed 2 changed file\(s\)'
stdout 'unlinked references \(1\)'
stdout 'README\.md:3\tdocs/guide\.md → docs/guide\.md'
stderr '1 lint issue\(s\) flagged'

# The inline-code path is the finding, and the real markdown link on the line
# below it is not — a link already registers as a graph edge, which is the
# whole point of converting the other one.
stdout 'unlinked references \(1\)'

# Every section says whether a human is needed. This one is auto-fixable and
# names the command that does it.
stdout 'fix \(auto\): run `semantic lint --fix`'

semantic lint --fix
stdout 'linked 1 unlinked reference\(s\) across 1 file\(s\)'
# The rewrite keeps the original path as the visible label and makes the target
# root-absolute, so the rendered page reads the same as before.
stdout 'README\.md\tdocs/guide\.md → \[`docs/guide\.md`\]\(/docs/guide\.md\)'
cmp README.md README.fixed

# Only the flagged reference moved. The fixer edits the span it found and
# leaves the rest of the file alone, including the link it deliberately did
# not flag.
semantic lint
stdout 'no lint issues flagged'
! stdout 'unlinked'

# Fixing changed a file, so the next run re-indexes exactly that one.
stderr 'reindexed 1 changed file\(s\)'

# Running the fixer with nothing to fix is a no-op that still succeeds, which
# is what makes it safe to wire into a hook unconditionally.
semantic lint --fix
! stdout 'linked 1'

-- README.md --
# Project

The guide lives in `docs/guide.md` and explains the rest.

See [the guide](docs/guide.md) for details.
-- docs/guide.md --
# Guide

Back to [the readme](../README.md).
-- README.fixed --
# Project

The guide lives in [`docs/guide.md`](/docs/guide.md) and explains the rest.

See [the guide](docs/guide.md) for details.
